\section{The Meaning of a Grammar Notation}

In a context-free grammar, the alternatives describe a union of possible derivations. In a parsing expression grammar, the alternatives describe a prioritized choice: the right alternative is tried only if the left one fails at that choice. A later failure outside a successful choice does not cause it to be reopened. Two notations can look almost identical and behave differently under extension. In particular, appending a production to a context-free grammar preserves all existing derivations if the old productions are retained; appending an ordered alternative need not add the intended new derivation.

Alessandro Warth and Ian Piumarta's \textit{OMeta: an Object-Oriented Language for Pattern Matching} extends parsing expressions to structured data and uses object-oriented mechanisms to extend grammars. Moritz Lenz's \textit{Parsing with Perl 6 Regexes and Grammars} shows a different rule-based system in which grammar rules are methods and can be reused through inheritance. These mechanisms make an override concise; they do not make it harmless. For an extension author, the contract must identify the overridable rules, the choice semantics, and the point at which a construct commits. The notation for repetition matters too: specify whether it can repeat a nullable item and whether trailing separators are accepted, rather than relying on the reader's intuition from another notation.

\section{Worked Example: Extending a Command}

Suppose a host accepts a command \texttt{show} followed by an identifier, as well as a bare identifier. An extension adds \texttt{show all}, which must become its own tree node; \texttt{show alloy} must remain an ordinary command with argument \texttt{alloy}. Work on tokens, with \texttt{SHOW} distinct from \texttt{ID}, and use \texttt{ALL} as shorthand for the predicate that a whole identifier token has spelling \texttt{all}. A grammar for complete lines can be written as follows, with slash denoting prioritized choice:
\begin{verbatim}
Line         <- Special End / Ordinary End / Name End
Special      <- SHOW ALL
Ordinary     <- SHOW ID
Name         <- ID
\end{verbatim}
On \texttt{show all}, both \texttt{Special} and \texttt{Ordinary} could consume both tokens, so the first complete alternative supplies the intended node. On \texttt{show alloy}, the spelling test fails and \texttt{Ordinary} succeeds. Using a globally reserved \texttt{ALL} token instead would also change bare \texttt{all}; using a character prefix test without a token boundary could match the start of \texttt{alloy}.

The placement of \texttt{End} is deliberate. If a later extension lets \texttt{Ordinary} accept a qualifier, \texttt{show all later} can still fall through to it when \texttt{Special End} fails. With \texttt{(Special / Ordinary / Name) End}, the choice would commit to the shorter \texttt{Special} before noticing the extra input. Switching between the two forms is therefore a language change, not just cleanup. To disallow qualifiers after \texttt{all}, a designer could instead make recognition of \texttt{show all} a documented commit point and diagnose extra input there.

\section{Composition Requires a Contract}

Markus Voelter and collaborators in \textit{DSL Engineering} discuss modular language design and integration. A practical extension point should state which parser and lexer mode own it, which tree category it may return, how siblings are ordered, and how an embedding ends. Where independent modules claim the same start tokens, check the combined grammar rather than trusting individual unit tests. Prefer rejecting a collision or requiring an explicit priority rule to silently depending on module load order.

Test an extension against a corpus of base programs and compare their normalized trees with and without it. Document intentional differences, such as the new tree for \texttt{show all}, alongside protected cases such as \texttt{show alloy} and the bare name \texttt{all}. Then test the module in combination with another module using the same hook, reversing installation order if order is meant to be irrelevant. Finally, keep recognition and binding contracts separate: the successful production determines shape, not necessarily the declaration to which a reference points. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-138} develops the symbol interface, while Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-137} considers what happens when an embedded construct is only half written.
